2. Student A – Exception Design
A. Throwable hierarchy
The relevant Java exception hierarchy is:
Throwable

Error

OutOfMemoryError
Serious JVM/system problems
Exception

IOException
RuntimeException
InputMismatchException
IllegalArgumentException
ArithmeticException
Program-level failures

InputMismatchException, IllegalArgumentException and ArithmeticException are subclasses of RuntimeException. IOException is a checked exception.

B. Exception classification table
Failure point	Exception	Type
Letters entered instead of numbers	InputMismatchException	Unchecked
Negative withdrawal amount	IllegalArgumentException	Unchecked
Withdrawal exceeds balance	ArithmeticException (used here as a custom choice)	Unchecked
Card-reader file missing	FileNotFoundException	Checked
File reading/closing problem	IOException	Checked
JVM runs out of memory	OutOfMemoryError	Error

C. Why is the catch block order important?
1. Specific exceptions should be caught first.
2. Broader exceptions should be caught later.
3. Multi-catch can handle multiple unrelated exception types together.
4. If a parent exception is caught first, its child exception catch block becomes unreachable.
Example:
catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Error");
}
catch (IOException e) {
    System.out.println("File error");
}


This produces a compilation error because IOException is already covered by Exception.
D. What does finally guarantee?
The finally block executes when control leaves the try/catch structure through normal completion or exception handling.

In this program, it displays the transaction completion message and closes the Scanner.

It generally executes even when an exception occurs, although abnormal JVM termination or certain process-level failures can prevent it.

